/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "216d9666-4724-5e2c-941b-bdc171687bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(e^{t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720337} :answer-field/id #uuid "92c8a934-ab58-51e8-a82e-1b27fa733481" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d82ecbae-43db-5155-8b5e-7f2b5f992602" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=e^{x}$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 y e^{- x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,e^{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,e^{t}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(e^{t}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 \\left(e^{t}\\right) e^{- t}\\\\\n&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponential factors cancel: $e^t e^{-t}=1$.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(e^{t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "15b32044-be8b-5d28-b3a6-300ac0efb837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720372} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "82a1dcc7-a7f5-5f76-bb68-2851ea39e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720380} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation." :answer/id #uuid "890e1cd9-220f-5e62-9b99-6009fed5ddbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720382} {:answer/feedback "The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives." :answer/id #uuid "e4761705-4ad3-59ca-b9ff-9ed95cc9e067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720396} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "fbd17bd0-1eb9-5a9f-832d-92d472a3b0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "82a1dcc7-a7f5-5f76-bb68-2851ea39e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720380} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cbaac3-1aff-5cf1-ba8e-831ddcd9dce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e3379fb-57b9-5bdb-bcd6-26c2c35cd8d3" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x e^{y} - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=3\\implies x=1\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=11\\implies x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} - 2 \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{3} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "4b35e920-e00f-5258-b979-b190b3179588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}\\left(t^{3} + t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720375} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, 